cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Analysis code for "Cortical and neuromuscular correlates of graded physical
  demand in human-exoskeleton interaction"
message: If you use this software, please cite both the software and the paper.
type: software
abstract: >-
  Analysis code for a mobile brain/body imaging (MoBI) study of how cortical and
  neuromuscular activity track graded physical demand in human-exoskeleton
  interaction, and of where the difficulty people report during effortful
  movement comes from. Fourteen
  participants performed a seated visuomotor pursuit-tracking task with the right
  leg while wearing a knee exoskeleton actuated by a pneumatic artificial muscle.
  The exoskeleton imposed demand at three supply pressures while the movement to
  be tracked was held constant, and every trial was rated for perceived
  difficulty. EEG, surface EMG and the experiment stream were recorded together
  over the Lab Streaming Layer. The repository covers the whole pipeline, from
  the raw recordings through preprocessing, independent component analysis and
  group clustering, to the reported figures and statistics. It holds the
  behavioural analysis and the mediation of perceived difficulty through muscular
  effort and tracking error, the cycle-resolved cluster time-frequency analysis,
  the coupling between parieto-occipital band power and tracking error, and a
  trial-level linear mixed model testing whether cortical band power explains
  perceived difficulty beyond effort and task performance. The derived data
  committed alongside the code reproduces the behavioural results, the coupling
  figure and the mixed model on a fresh clone with nothing downloaded. Written in
  MATLAB, building on EEGLAB and the BeMoBIL pipeline. It accompanies a
  manuscript in preparation.
authors:
